# Spanish and English glyph coverage and fallback

## Scope and existing evidence

Connect the existing Spanish/English grapheme inventory, admitted educational
symbols, NFC/NFD accents, Spanish guillemets and punctuation, and custom glyph
fallback to Rust rendering.

The historical accepted theoretical design is preserved in completed P1–P9
records. This record is **open executable integration work**. It must not be
closed merely because a prior design was accepted or a different
implementation was proposed.

## Owner's glyph geometry target (unfinished)

Start from exact reusable geometric primitives, not a collection of random
raster marks. A lowercase **o** is a true circle with center and radius;
a simple single-storey **a** reuses the loop plus a stem. The two-storey
printed **a** is a separate outline, alongside italic and cursive variants.

Allow the user to edit and preview ordered individual pen strokes, choose
each stroke direction, and reverse it explicitly. Add precisely anchored
accents (especially á, é, í, ó, ú), ñ and ü; their placement and stroke
sequence are visible and tunable. The canonical glyph stays unchanged as
width, height, thickness, slant, spacing and regularity are adjusted.

Current saved segment geometry does not establish an analytic-circle glyph
primitive, a built-in two-storey alphabet or automatic joined cursive. These
requirements remain open until visibly rendered and user-editable.

## Executable inventory-only progress — 2026-11-03

A real Firefox session saves a Rust handwriting profile with an exact `ñ`
override. The separate Rust CLI reopens that browser-saved file and audits
`¿Qué? ¡Pingüino! «ñ» ñ ∞` by exact grapheme, preserving the distinction
between composed `ñ`, decomposed `n` plus combining tilde, and an external
math symbol. The test verifies that `ñ` reports a statistical override,
decomposed `ñ` inherits global statistics, and `∞` is explicitly outside
the frozen English/Spanish **text** inventory.

The CLI clearly states that inventory membership **does not imply
renderability**. No real stroke geometry or user-selected visible fallback
is supplied for `∞` or any other grapheme, so the task remains open until
the fixed multilingual page can render or present exact missing shapes with
an honest, user-owned fallback. This evidence does not imply full Unicode
coverage, automatic handwriting, or a completed font/shape engine.

**Test:** `Firefox edits, saves and reopens both profiles through Rust
localhost` in
`tests/browser/workspace/adapter-inbound/runtime-frame-ancestors.mjs`.

## CLI exact-glyph ink fallback (partial) — 2026-11-03

The Rust `atrament-profiles notebook` CLI now has a bounded caller-owned
shape path: repeated `--glyph-stroke` segments for one exact grapheme,
followed by `--place-text` to render those shapes on a selected PDF/PNG
page. The same native compositor handles pen widths, colors, printable
bounds and page isolation. Every nonspace grapheme must have at least one
explicit authored stroke; all missing exact graphemes and their U+ scalar
sequences are reported before file replacement. A focused test covers
composed `ñ`, decomposed `ñ` and custom mathematical `∞`, and verifies
byte-identical PDF/PNG ink against explicit `--ink-line` strokes.

The native CLI now optionally reads a saved handwriting statistics profile
(`--handwriting-profile ink.json`) to apply six per-grapheme mean variables
(width, height, ink thickness, letter spacing, baseline offset and slant) to
those explicit caller-drawn segments. The 3.5 × 4.5 mm template
cell is mapped to physical strokes, including inherited global and exact
glyph overrides.

Negative advances, invalid pen widths and nonprintable placements refuse.
Slant uses the configured mean in millidegrees to shear the glyph about its
lower baseline, leaning its top right for positive values or left for
negative ones. Out-of-page slanted strokes refuse before file replacement.
This is only a deterministic mean-based geometric transform; no sampled
variation, real glyph capture or synthesis is claimed.

The built-in 114-entry inventory is still **not a set of actual outlines**.
No automatically selected glyphs, calibrated forms, statistical ink or
persisted glyph templates exist. This partial caller-drawn fallback does
not establish full P4-012 coverage; this task remains open.

## Explicit caller-selected fallback (partial) — 2026-11-03

The native notebook PDF/PNG CLI now accepts repeated
`--fallback-glyph MISSING AUTHORED` for at most 64 exact Unicode
single-grapheme mappings. Only a genuinely missing source can borrow a
present caller-authored target; duplicates, chains and missing targets
refuse. A composed `ñ` can explicitly borrow decomposed `ñ` stroke
segments without pretending the two Unicode graphemes are identical.
The rendering stage retains the original grapheme's statistical means
for scaling and slant, and preserves the actual authored segment colors.

Four focused regressions compare direct-substitution PDF/PNG byte output,
verify the original statistical override, reject missing or ambiguous
mappings, and preserve an existing selected PDF after a refused export.
Fallbacks are per export, not serialized in the stroke library or exposed
in the browser; their presence does not imply the 114 baseline glyphs
have real outlines. This task remains open.

## Local browser geometry editor (partial) — 2026-11-03

After an admitted localhost session, the browser now has an exact Unicode
per-glyph stroke editor. Author line segments using native-bounded numeric
fields and sliders, inspect an SVG stroke preview, load a bounded LF UTF-8
versioned TSV, and explicitly download the Rust CLI-compatible TSV. Session
reset clears the in-memory draft, and input refusal does not replace it.

The browser performs bounded input checks, but the Rust CLI remains the file
and PDF/PNG admission authority. Separate browser tests prove exact NFC/NFD
and math Unicode roundtrips, bad-file refusal, preview/download gestures,
and stale asynchronous import rejection. The Rust session runtime serves the
new JavaScript from its explicit allowlist and tests the served bytes.

The browser now selects any authored segment by dropdown or SVG line,
preloads its exact physical coordinates and ink color, and applies validated
replacement or targeted deletion without reconstructing other segments.
Invalid changes preserve the previous library. Focused frontend tests cover
mixed-grapheme imports, middle-segment editing, wrong-length refusal and
explicit TSV output.

Direct SVG pointer drawing now records up to 128 connected native-unit
segments per gesture, with canceled and oversized traces refusing atomically.
The editor can explicitly copy a source glyph's authored strokes into a
separate exact Unicode target, then independently edit those segments.
Browser and Rust CLI glyph library caps are now 4096 authored strokes and
512 KiB; a real 600-stroke mixed composed/decomposed Unicode library passes
byte-identical Rust readback. Rendered text still has its distinct bound
of 512 physical ink lines, and no baseline outlines are auto-generated.

Not yet implemented: automatically generating the entire required 114-glyph
baseline, browser-native notebook ink composition, and learned writer
calibration. This task remains open.

## Analytic o/simple-a browser starter (partial) — 2026-11-03

The localhost authoring surface now exposes editable center/radius inputs,
with a true SVG circle guide for `o` and that same circle plus a right stem
for single-storey `a`. An explicit action converts the construction into
48 (or 49) integer-micrometre segments for the existing Rust v1 TSV format.
A Rust CLI roundtrip accepted the generated sample without changing bytes;
real single-page native PDF and transparent PNG were exported with `oa`.

Browser regressions cover o/a shared-loop geometry, path closure, exact
Unicode replacement, and refusal of invalid dimensions. The preview guide
is **exact SVG geometry**, but the exported segments only approximate its
circle; no analytic primitive persists to PDF/PNG. Two-storey a, cursive,
accent anchors, and the full bilingual inventory are still open.

## Acceptance decision

Open. Provide the actual bounded Rust/backend or browser integration and an
accepted/refused example before marking the executable capability complete.
Theoretical design acceptance alone cannot establish that this feature runs.
